name mig29-opt-refused-1
timeout 200

# WHERE THE DIRECTOR SAYS NO — and it says no for a reason that is one documented number, not a rule
# somebody wrote (doc/modules/mig29/weapons.md §5.4.2, refusal 1).
#
# THE CHAIN, and every link of it is sourced:
#   the trigger may only be pressed 1...10 s after the rangefinder answered  [DCS-EA p.99, p.101]
#   the countdown then has to be long enough for the release to be flown     [DCS-EA p.101 step 8]
#   so the ranging act has to happen SEVERAL KILOMETRES before the release point
#   and the only ranging device this aircraft has reaches 6 km               [doc/sensors.md §6]
#   while the release point sits one BOMB-THROW short of the target
#   and the throw grows with altitude
#   => there is an altitude above which a level delivery cannot be set up at all.
#
# NOBODY DECIDED THAT CEILING. This file measures it, by bracketing it with two aircraft that differ in
# exactly one line — their run-in altitude:
#
#   MEASURED (2026-07-30, --elev const), FAB-500 at 450 kt:
#     marginal  2000 m  one LRF_NO_RETURN, then a range at 5760 m and a countdown of only 2.10 s
#                       -> release taken, `sms RELEASE`, the last altitude that still works
#     refused   2200 m  two LRF_NO_RETURN (6484 m and 6009 m, both beyond the 6 km reach), then a range
#                       at 5539 m that comes too late: `director DIRECTOR_REFUSED
#                       reason=no_release_point` and `pilot ATTACK_BREAKOFF why="release point passed"`
#                       in the same tick, and NO `sms RELEASE` line anywhere for this jet
#     -> the level-bombing ceiling of this airframe lies between 2.0 and 2.2 km ASL. Above it the
#        aircraft carries the bombs and cannot aim them.
#
# THAT IS THE ANSWER TO O3's ANCHOR SENTENCE. "Su-7 and MiG-17 fighter-bombers came in very low" is not
# a mission author's stylistic choice in this tree: it is what a 6 km rangefinder forces on anybody
# using this delivery. A striker that climbs to stay out of small-arms range loses the ability to
# deliver at all, and it loses it at a measured altitude.
#
# WHAT THE REFUSAL IS NOT: it is not a missing feature and not an error path. `DCS-EA p.101` gives a
# procedure for "sighting angle equals release angle" (release at once) and for "release angle greater
# than sighting angle" (the countdown). For the third case — the release point already behind — it
# gives none, because there is nothing to fly to. The director returns that third case verbatim.
#
# READING RULE: exit 0 = SUCCESS is bookkeeping only (both jets declare `survive` and both survive);
# it is NOT the measurement. THE MEASUREMENT IS THE ABSENCE of a `sms RELEASE` line for `refused` and
# the presence of `director DIRECTOR_REFUSED reason=no_release_point`, read beside `marginal`'s
# successful release on the same geometry 200 m lower. Neither aircraft declares a kill objective: this
# file is about whether a delivery can be SET UP, not about what it destroys.

unit marginal
  module mig29
  team hostile
  spawn 46.90000 6.70000 2000 90.0 450       # 26 km of run-in: the laser gate, not the leg, is the limit
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 60
  set n019_emission off
  set store 3 fab500
  set store 6 fab500
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task attack
  set attack_mode opt
  wp 46.90000 7.05000 2000 450
  wp 46.95000 7.30000 2000 400
  objective survive

unit marginal_tgt
  module target_soft
  team friendly
  spawn 46.90000 7.05000 ground 90.0 0

unit refused
  module mig29
  team hostile
  spawn 46.80000 6.70000 2200 90.0 450       # the ONE changed line: 200 m higher
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 60
  set n019_emission off
  set store 3 fab500
  set store 6 fab500
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task attack
  set attack_mode opt
  wp 46.80000 7.05000 2200 450
  wp 46.85000 7.30000 2200 400
  objective survive

unit refused_tgt
  module target_soft
  team friendly
  spawn 46.80000 7.05000 ground 90.0 0
